    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">Here&#39;s a Q &amp; D way to do it - I named your data DF to avoid any conflicts with commonly occurring functions:<div><br></div><div><div>m &lt;- lm(Sales2 ~ Sales1, data = DF)</div><div>mc &lt;- round(coef(m), 2)</div>\r\n<div>strg &lt;- paste0(&quot;hat(y) == &quot;,mc[1], &quot; + &quot;, mc[2], &quot;~Sales1&quot;)</div><div><br></div><div>require(ggplot2)</div><div>ggplot(DF, aes(x = Sales1, y = Sales2)) + </div><div>    geom_point(size=3) + </div>\r\n<div>    stat_smooth(method = &quot;lm&quot;, se = FALSE) +</div><div>    geom_text( x = 200, y = 2400, </div><div>              label = strg, parse = TRUE)</div><div><br></div><div>For textual annotation in ggplot2, the plotmath expression has to be expressed as a character string, and this needs to be done outside of ggplot2. So the game is to fit the model, extract the coefficients and then use a paste function to construct a string representation of the plotmath expression. In geom_text(), set the coordinates at which the equation should be plotted, use the name of the string as the value of the label argument and declare parse = TRUE, which will parse and evaluate the string inside ggplot2. If you want to do this for each of several panels, see here:</div>\r\n<div><a href=\"https://groups.google.com/forum/#!topic/ggplot2/H4Gd8c10GN4\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://groups.google.com/forum/%23!topic/ggplot2/H4Gd8c10GN4&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792371180291000&amp;usg=AOvVaw0nzj8VV6-RgrsfqkmzUxf_\">https://groups.google.com/forum/#!topic/ggplot2/H4Gd8c10GN4</a><br></div><div><br></div><div><br></div><div><br></div><div>Dennis</div><div><br></div>\r\n<div><br></div></div></div><div class=\"gmail_extra\"><br><br><div class=\"gmail_quote\"></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div class=\"gmail_extra\"><div class=\"gmail_quote\">On Fri, Mar 7, 2014 at 10:41 AM, JOH JOH <span dir=\"ltr\">&lt;<a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">renalcl...@gmail.com</a>&gt;</span> wrote:<br>\r\n</div></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div class=\"gmail_extra\"><div class=\"gmail_quote\"><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0 0 0 .8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\">Could someone advise how to do a regression line for the following data and code?  I used the <font face=\"courier new, monospace\">stat_smooth(method=&quot;lm&quot;)</font>code but would like to see the equation r2 etc with don&#39;t really need the 5&amp;95th percentiles.  Data and code included.<div>\r\n\r\n<br></div><div>Thank you,</div><div>JOH</div><div><br></div><div><font face=\"courier new, monospace\">ggplot(dt, aes(Sales1, Sales2))+geom_point(aes(colour = factor(Store)),size=3, alpha=1)+ stat_smooth(method = &quot;lm&quot;)</font><br>\r\n\r\n</div><div><font face=\"courier new, monospace\"><br></font></div><div>\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n<span><font color=\"#888888\">\r\n </font></span><span><font color=\"#888888\">\r\n\r\n</font></span><table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\" width=\"195\" style=\"border-collapse:collapse;width:195pt\">\r\n\r\n <colgroup><col width=\"65\" span=\"3\" style=\"width:65pt\">\r\n </colgroup><tbody><tr height=\"15\" style=\"height:15pt\">\r\n  <td height=\"15\" width=\"65\" style=\"height:15pt;width:65pt\"><font face=\"courier new, monospace\">Store</font></td>\r\n  <td width=\"65\" style=\"width:65pt\"><font face=\"courier new, monospace\">Sales1</font></td>\r\n  <td width=\"65\" style=\"width:65pt\"><font face=\"courier new, monospace\">Sales2</font></td>\r\n </tr>\r\n <tr height=\"15\" style=\"height:15pt\">\r\n  <td height=\"15\" align=\"right\" style=\"height:15pt\"><font face=\"courier new, monospace\">1</font></td>\r\n  <td align=\"right\"><font face=\"courier new, monospace\">513</font></td>\r\n  <td align=\"right\"><font face=\"courier new, monospace\">1077.3</font></td>\r\n </tr>\r\n <tr height=\"15\" style=\"height:15pt\">\r\n  <td height=\"15\" align=\"right\" style=\"height:15pt\"><font face=\"courier new, 